You are about to connect this agent to DataCodex Notify, a local app that sends WhatsApp notifications from long sessions. Do exactly the following, once, and do not do anything else.

1. Open this rules file: `GEMINI.md` inside the `.gemini` folder of the user's home folder (create it if it does not exist).
2. Write the block below into that file. The block starts with the line `<!-- datacodex-notify:start v1 -->` and ends with the line `<!-- datacodex-notify:end -->`. If the file already contains these markers, replace everything from the start marker to the end marker (both lines included) with the new block. Never add a second block, and do not change anything outside the markers.
3. Do not run any `datacodex-notify` command now. Notifications stay OFF until they are activated as the block describes.
4. Reply with the absolute path of every file you changed, and nothing else.

The block to write (copy it exactly, including the two marker lines):

````markdown
<!-- datacodex-notify:start v1 -->
## DataCodex Notify (WhatsApp notifications)

**Who this applies to.** Only the main (manager or reviewer) session follows this block. If you are a subagent, an implementer, a delegate, or any session started by another agent, ignore this whole block and never call DataCodex Notify.

**Tool.** The console program is `C:\Users\demo\AppData\Local\Programs\DatacodexNotify\datacodex-notify-cli.exe`. Always call it by this full path (it is not on PATH).
Commands: `send --level info|warning|critical --text-file <file> --source gemini --session <name> [--project <folder name>] [--title <text>] [--field key=value] [--mode text|voice|both] [--wait <seconds>]`, `connect --source gemini --session <name> --project <folder name> --rules-file <absolute path> [--rules-file <absolute path>]`, `disconnect --source gemini --session <name>`, `heartbeat --id <name> --every <seconds> --grace <seconds> --label "<label>" --source gemini --session <name>`, `heartbeat-stop --id <name>`.
Always write the message to a UTF-8 file without a BOM and pass it with `--text-file`; never type Arabic directly on the command line.

### One-off message or task activation
There are two different cases. Never mix them up.
- **A one-off message the user asks for directly** (for example "send me a test message" or "message me when this is done"): run only `send` with `--wait 20`. Do **not** run `connect`, `disconnect`, or `heartbeat` for it. Add `--session <name>` only if a session is already active in this conversation. Never connect and disconnect around a single message.
- **Activating notifications for a task or a conversation**: `connect` **once**, as described below, then use the same session name for the whole conversation. Disconnect only when the user says a deactivation phrase or when the task ends.

### Switching notifications on and off
Notifications are **OFF** for every session until they are activated.
- Activate only when the user asks, for example with one of: "notify on", "start alerts".
- For a long task you may suggest once, in a single short line, that notifications can be turned on. Do not suggest again in the same session, and never turn them on yourself.
- Deactivate when the user says one of: "notify off", "stop alerts".

**To activate:**
1. Pick a unique session name once: the project name plus a short random suffix, for example `myproject-k7f2`. Reuse exactly this name for the whole conversation; never pick a new one.
2. Run `connect` with `--source gemini`, that session name, `--project` with the project name (see "The project name" below), and `--rules-file` with the absolute path of every rules file that contains this block (the file you are reading now, and any other copy you know of).
3. If the program exits with code 6, notifications were turned off by the user: stop here and do not try again in this session.
4. Start the heartbeat (see below).

**To deactivate** (only on a deactivation phrase, or when the task ends): stop the heartbeat, run `disconnect` once with the same session name, and send nothing more in this session.

### What to report
| Event | Level | Mode flag |
|-------|-------|-----------|
| A task starts (include a short plan summary and the number of steps) | info | --mode text |
| The task is finished (short summary and the next step) | info | --mode text |
| A build or test fails, or an error stops progress | warning | --mode text |
| You are blocked or need the user's approval to continue | warning | --mode text |
| A stop condition: three failed builds in a row, a broken core feature, or a security problem | critical | --mode both |

Do not report anything else. Always pass `--session` with your session name; the session remembers the project, so only `connect` needs `--project`. A one-off `send` without a session may pass `--project <folder name>` itself. Messages show the agent and the project in their header.

### The project name
`--project` is the name of the git repository root folder of the work you are doing (the last part of `git rev-parse --show-toplevel`). If the work is not in a git repository, use the name of the working folder. If that name looks temporary (a temp or cache folder, a random or generated name, a pasted-files folder) or there is no real project, leave `--project` out.

### Writing the messages
- Write every notification in short Egyptian Arabic (colloquial, plain words).
- Keep the body to one to three lines: what happened and what you need from the user, if anything.
- Use `--title` for a one-line summary (up to 120 characters) and `--field "key=value"` (repeatable, at most 8, key up to 40 and value up to 200 characters) for short facts such as the phase, the test result, or the step. Do not put formatting marks (`*`, `_`, `~`, backticks) in a title or a field; they are shown as plain text.
- Never include code, file contents, secrets, tokens, passwords, or customer data.
- Use `--source gemini` on every command, the same value you gave to `connect`. The project is already in the session name.

### Never let notifications get in the way
A failed notification must never fail, block, or delay your work.
- Exit 0: sent (or held on purpose). Exit 5: duplicate or rate limited; not an error.
- Exit 2: the app is not running. Continue the task and mention it once in your final report.
- Exit 3: authentication failed. Continue and mention it in your final report. Do not try to fix it.
- Exit 4: the command was invalid. Fix it and try once more.
- Exit 7: only with `--wait`: the message is still queued after the wait. Not an error; continue.
- Exit 6: the user turned notifications off. Stop sending notifications for the rest of this session and do not retry.
Do not change the app, its settings, or its files.

### Heartbeat
After activating, run `heartbeat --id <session name> --every 600 --grace 300 --label "<project name>" --source gemini --session <session name>`.
While you work, run `heartbeat --id <session name> --session <session name>` again at least every 600 seconds (for example after every step). If you stop reporting in, the user is alerted.
When the task ends, or when you deactivate, run `heartbeat-stop --id <session name>`.
<!-- datacodex-notify:end -->

````
